A||||5||sqoach||what's wrong with my temp guage?||||11||
Z||000000||sqoach||12-06-2000||07:15 PM||||yet another problem with my car. the temp guage if fooked up. when i first start my car, it does ok. then the needle goes way up, then comes down, and it just moves around a lot. it's not jerky movement, it moves pretty smooth. could be temp sensor? or need new gauge? thanks.<P>( i'm sure evry1's gettin tired of responding to my threads, but don't you just like helping a fellow fiero owner  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )<BR><P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>fiero7@hotmail.com<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_2.JPG"> ||||reg||11
Z||000001||Old Lar||12-06-2000||08:19 PM||||Cycling sounds like the radiator fan is coming on and off as the coolant heats up.  The radiator cools the antifreeze, the temp drops, the fan kicks off.  Temp goes back up  etc.  Does this happen at idle?  Does this happen as you cruise down the road?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-06-2000||08:31 PM||||You could have air in the system too. If the air pocket is moving the car will heat up when it hits the water pump. As it gets hotter the coolant expands catches water pump and it cools the car down. Sounds like you need to burp the cooling system.||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||12-06-2000||09:20 PM||||old lar, it doesn't have anything to do with the fan. i had the hood open to see if the fan comes on while the temp gauge goes up and it doesn't turn on. and the temp would not drop/rise so dramatically in such short time. my fan does work though. i turn on the a/c and it kicks on. i turn a/c off, turns fan off.<BR>and oreif, i'm sure i burped it before. maybe i should try it again. i'm kinding thinking that might be it too. i noticed the coolant flowing into the overflow tank after i turned my car off. i checked to see if the hoses were hot or even warm and they were not. so it could be the pressure cap is not working right. but i'll try the burping method first.<BR>can you tell me exactly how to do this please? because i must have done something wrong when i tried it. thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tigger||12-06-2000||09:59 PM||||It could be a sticking thermostat too.  The gauge temp sensor is located at the base of the filler neck/thermostat housing.  The temperature gauge might be rising on the dash because the coolant isn't flowing past the thermostat.  When the thermostat pops, the temp drops in a hurry.<P>You don't really have to bother with testing it because they are inexpensive and easy to replace, but here goes...<P>1. Remove the thermostat from the housing, under the cap.<BR>2. Place the thermostat so that it's submerged a pan of water on a stove.<BR>3. Heat the water to 195 F or whatever is stamped for the rating on the bottom of the thermostat.  Use a cooking thermometer to watch the temp.  If it takes a much higher water temperature to make it pop, then it's bad.<BR>4. Allow it to cool, it should close again after dropping 10-20 F below the rating.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||12-07-2000||02:36 AM||||To "burp" a Fiero, Raise back of car up (or park nose down a driveway) remove cap for thermostat in back. Then SLOWLY loosen the front radiator cap. If you hear hissing, you have air in the system. Loosen a little more until coolant come out. Tighten cap go to back, pull thermostat, check level, If low top off. (You can check thermostat then)<BR>Install thermostat and cap then drive around and see what happens.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
